+# Nano-Forth
+
+A cross-platform, minimalistic Forth-based environment targeting DOS/16-bit x86.
+
+## Project Overview
+
+- **Language**: x86 assembly (NASM/TASM syntax) + Forth source
+- **Target**: DOS COM programs (16-bit real mode, CP/M-style `.COM` format, `org 0100h`)
+- **Forth dialect**: F83-based with custom extensions
+- **Architecture**: Indirect-threaded code (ITC), register `SI` as instruction pointer, `BX` as top-of-stack, `BP` as return stack pointer
+
+## Key Files
+
+| File | Purpose |
+|---|---|
+| `itsy.asm` | Minimal Forth kernel in x86 assembly (primitives, inner interpreter) |
+| `mini/FRT.SCR` | Main Forth source — compiler, editor, target compiler (Forth screens, 1024-byte blocks) |
+| `mini/TCOMP2.SCR` | Target/cross compiler screens |
+| `mini/MINI.COM` | Compiled minimal Forth system |
+| `F.EXE` / `F.INI` | Host Forth interpreter |
+| `TASM.EXE` / `TLINK.EXE` | Turbo Assembler + Linker (build tools) |
+| `PE2.EXE` | Screen/block editor |
+| `4TH.SCR`, `4TH3.SCR` | Additional Forth screens |
+
+## Build
+
+Assembly is done with Turbo Assembler:
+```
+TASM itsy.asm
+TLINK itsy.obj /t   (produces .COM)
+```
+
+Forth source is loaded via screen blocks (1024-byte fixed-width files, classic Forth block format). Use `THRU` and `LOAD` to compile screens.
+
+## Forth Screen Format
+
+`.SCR` files are raw binary, 1024 bytes per screen (block), 16 lines × 64 chars each. Not line-terminated — fixed width padding with spaces.
+
+## Architecture Notes
+
+- **Inner interpreter** (`next`): `lodsw` / `xchg di,ax` / `jmp word[di]` — classic ITC
+- **Return stack**: grows down from `SP=-256`, pointer in `BP`
+- **Data stack**: x86 hardware stack; TOS in `BX`
+- **Dictionary**: linked list, last entry in `last` variable
+- **Macros**: `primitive`, `colon`, `variable`, `constant` defined in `macros.asm`
+- **Vocabularies**: F83-style `ALSO`/`PREVIOUS`/`DEFINITIONS`
+
+## Conventions
+
+- Forth words use lowercase labels with underscores internally (e.g., `xt_abort`, `val_state`)
+- Immediate words flagged with high bit (0x80) in name length byte
+- `(;code)` / `do_semi_code` used for `DOES>` and defining words
+- Target compiler (`TCOMP`) aliases host Forth words with `T-` prefix for cross-compilation
